<comment datatype='string'>Catabolic enoyl-CoA hydratases (ECHs) are important enzymes whose best known role is participation in the &amp;beta;-oxidation pathway of fatty acids. ECH catalyzes the second step, the hydration of a &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=TRANS-D2-ENOYL-COA&quot;&gt;a (2&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-2-enoyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; to a &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=L-3-HYDROXYACYL-COA&quot; title=&quot;&lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=ACYL-COA''&gt;Acyl-CoAs&lt;/a&gt; are oxoacids that have been activated by &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CO-A''&gt;coenzyme A&lt;/a&gt;. They are often classified by the length of their acyl moiety.&amp;#10;&amp;#10;&lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Medium-Chain-Acyl-CoAs''&gt;Medium-chain acyl-CoAs&lt;/a&gt;  are derived from fatty acids with aliphatic tails of 6-12 carbons.&amp;#10;&amp;#10;&lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Long-Chain-Acyl-CoAs''&gt;Long-chain acyl-CoAs&lt;/a&gt; are derived from fatty acids with aliphatic tails of 13-21 carbons.&amp;#10;&amp;#10;&lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=VERY-LONG-CHAIN-FATTY-ACYL-COA''&gt;Very long-chain fatty acyl-CoAs&lt;/a&gt; are derived from fatty acids with aliphatic tails of 22 carbons or longer.&quot;&gt;a 2,3,4-saturated (3&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-3-hydroxyacyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; (see &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=FAO-PWY&quot;&gt;fatty acid &amp;beta;-oxidation I (generic)&lt;/a&gt;).
&lt;p&gt;
Two types of catabolic ECH enzymes are known: the peroxisomal ECHs, as well as bacterial enzymes such as the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-83333&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt; K-12&lt;/a&gt; FadB, are parts of multifunctional complexes that have three or four activities, including ECH [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/3286611&quot;&gt;Yang88&lt;/a&gt;]. The mitochondrial enzymes, on the other hand, are homohexameric, single function enzymes [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12467702&quot;&gt;Agnihotri03&lt;/a&gt;]. Analogs of the mitochondrial enzyme have been discovered in bacteria. For example, two such enzymes, one of which homodimeric, were described in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1772&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Mycolicibacterium smegmatis&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/7451404&quot;&gt;Fujita80&lt;/a&gt;]. A gene homologous to the mitochondrial ECH was discovered in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1061&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Rhodobacter capsulatus&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/1743516&quot;&gt;Beckman91&lt;/a&gt;] and homologous genes are present in many bacteria.
&lt;p&gt;
Although mammalian ECH enzymes have been studied extensively (for review, see [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12467702&quot;&gt;Agnihotri03&lt;/a&gt;]), surprisingly little is known about the bacterial catabolic enzymes (more is known about the bacterial biosynthetic enzymes involved in lipid or polyhydroxyalkanoate biosynthesis - see [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10713420&quot;&gt;Tsuge00&lt;/a&gt;] and [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12115060&quot;&gt;Fiedler02&lt;/a&gt;]).
&lt;p&gt;
An ECH enzyme involved in the catabolism of branched-chain amino acids has been characterized and partially purified from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-303&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas putida&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; by Conrad et al [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4150713&quot;&gt;Conrad74&lt;/a&gt;]. The enzyme was induced during growth on &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ILE&quot;&gt;L-isoleucine&lt;/a&gt;, and active with both &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CROTONYL-COA&quot;&gt;crotonyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-1083&quot;&gt;(&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-2-methylcrotonoyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;, but not with &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=TRANS-3-METHYL-GLUTACONYL-COA&quot;&gt;3-methylglutaconyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;, suggesting it may not be involved in &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=LEU&quot;&gt;L-leucine&lt;/a&gt; degradation. The enzyme was further characterized by Roberts et al, who found that the enzyme was most active with &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CROTONYL-COA&quot;&gt;crotonyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;, had significant activity with &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-1083&quot;&gt;(&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-2-methylcrotonoyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; and rather low activity with &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=3-METHYL-CROTONYL-COA&quot;&gt;3-methylcrotonyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;, another intermediate in &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=LEU&quot;&gt;L-leucine&lt;/a&gt; degradation [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/678016&quot;&gt;Roberts78&lt;/a&gt;]. They concluded that the enzyme participates in both fatty acid oxidation and the metabolism of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ILE&quot;&gt;L-isoleucine&lt;/a&gt;, and potentially also in the degradation of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=VAL&quot;&gt;L-valine&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/678016&quot;&gt;Roberts78&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
A &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=MONOMER-11695&quot;&gt;similar enzyme&lt;/a&gt; was described by Puukka in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-294&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas fluorescens&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; during growth on &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=VAL&quot;&gt;L-valine&lt;/a&gt;, that converted &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=METHACRYLYL-COA&quot;&gt;methylacrylyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=3-HYDROXY-ISOBUTYRYL-COA&quot;&gt;3-hydroxy-isobutanoyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4633993&quot;&gt;Puukka73&lt;/a&gt;]. It is not known if that enzyme was also involved in the degradation of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ILE&quot;&gt;L-isoleucine&lt;/a&gt; since its specificity has not been characterized. However, it was best induced by growth on &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ILE&quot;&gt;L-isoleucine&lt;/a&gt;.</comment>
